# Narrative Options — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Author:** creative-director
**Date:** 2026-08-10
**Inputs:** run prompt, run manifest (schema 2.0, 14 selected research agents), `outputs/stage1/evidence-map.md`, `outputs/evidence-ledger.jsonl` (262 records, spot-verified against curator IDs)

The evidence base is strong and convergent: an intelligibility floor already
codified (NFPA 72, ACRP 175, IEC 60268-16), a Design Manual that is already the
binding vehicle, a procurement window closing with the Concourse C/D and DCA
Terminal 1 scopes, and a peer record whose mechanisms are more transferable
than its numbers. The danger is that the Strategist writes the obvious report:
"specify early, here are the peer airports, here is a tiered table." Every
reader in this audience has seen that deck. These three options change the
angle of approach without changing a single fact.

All three options lead to the same decision — a Terminal Acoustic and
Information Performance Standard adopted as a Design Manual amendment on the
current CCP procurement clock (evidence-map §10) — because that is the only
decision the ledger carries. What differs is the door the reader walks
through, the order of the argument, and which chair at the table the article
is quietly written for.

---

## Option 1 — Board-ready: "The $6.8 Million Paragraph"

*Direct, conservative, decision-led. The article the CFO forwards.*

### Thesis (one sentence)

MWAA should adopt a Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard as
a Design Manual amendment now — before the Concourse C/D and DCA Terminal 1
consultant scopes harden — because the requirements are already defined in
code and research, the cost is 0.06–0.20% of the announced IAD program, and
every month of delay converts a specification line into a change order.

### Communication job

By the end, the MWAA Board and executive leadership should authorize drafting
the Design Manual amendment within the current Board officer term, because the
standard costs a rounding error when written into the RFP and a rate-hearing
fight when written after it.

### Opening scene

Not a terminal — a spreadsheet. Open on the two numbers the Board already
stares at: signatory CPE at IAD forecast to rise from $12.77 to $20.71 by
2030, and debt service coverage compressing from 1.63× toward roughly 1.3×
as $5.5B of new debt phases in (E-0003, E-0026). Then the pivot: inside that
envelope, there is exactly one category of capital decision that gets cheaper
the earlier it is made, and MWAA is about to procure 5 million square feet of
it.

### Argument sequence (six beats)

1. **The window.** The July 2026 Dulles announcement (E-0005, E-0006, E-0084)
   and Concourse E's fall 2026 opening (E-0227) mean every future terminal at
   both airports will be scoped inside the next standards cycle. Standards
   adopted late get argued as change orders.
2. **The requirements already exist.** NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 average / ≥ 0.45 at
   90% of points (E-0073, E-0074); ACRP 175 targets and SNR ≥ 10 dB(A)
   (E-0019, E-0040, E-0222); IEC 60268-16 STIPA as the acceptance test
   (E-0042, E-0052). MWAA is not being asked to invent — it is being asked to
   bind.
3. **The vehicle already exists.** The seven-volume 2020 Design Manual is "a
   mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property" (E-0029,
   E-0108, E-0143, E-0158, E-0164, E-0226 — six-agent corroboration). No new
   policy instrument; an amendment plus a Specification section.
4. **The arithmetic.** Acoustic ceiling $5–15/sf new vs. $18–45/sf retrofit;
   avoided rework on Concourse E alone $1.3M–$17.4M, midpoint $6.8M (Exhibit
   E1); full MWAA-wide stack $12M–$40M one-time, 0.06–0.20% of the announced
   IAD program, $0.03–$0.11 per enplanement (Exhibit E6). Use the Quant
   Analyst's 2×–4× late-spec multiplier, not the 1:10:100 heuristic.
5. **The cautionary precedents.** BER's smoke-extraction and voice-notification
   integration that never certified against a frozen design, €2B → ~€7.3B and
   nine years late (E-0011, E-0012, E-0013); DIA's automated baggage
   (E-0014, E-0015). Direction uncontested; cite as direction, not multiplier.
6. **The airline-consent timing.** Adopted inside the Design Manual before
   consultant scopes harden, the standard rides inside existing fees and does
   not read as a rate change; adopted after, it is an MII-adjacent fight with
   United and American as anchors (E-0001, E-0109, E-0114, E-0115; do not
   cite specific MII percentages — not verified). The window is the current
   Board officer term.

### Strongest objection and where it enters

The Contrarian's: NFPA 72 + ADA/ACAA + ACRP 175 + a competent commissioning
spec already require everything the standard would require, so the amendment
is governance surface, not new requirement — and the Board has not asked for
this. It enters at beat 3 and must be answered head-on: the requirement lives
in *integration and enforcement* — commissioning acceptance in occupied
conditions, re-verification after tenant fit-out, tenant-audio scope — which
no individual code delivers.

### Analogy

One only if it earns its place: standards adopted before procurement are
specification lines; adopted after, they are change orders — the same words
at a different price. (Skip cross-domain analogies here; this option's power
is its refusal to decorate.)

### Signature visual

**"The cost of the same sentence, by adoption date."** A single horizontal
timeline from today through C/D RFP award, with the identical STI/SNR
requirement priced at three points: in the Design Manual now (~0.1% of
program), in the RFP addendum (2×–4×), post-award change order (Concourse E
retrofit band, $18–45/sf). One requirement, three prices, one closing window.

### The airport decision it leads toward

Board authorization, this officer term, for the Office of Engineering to
draft the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard as a Design
Manual amendment with companion Specification and Estimating & Scheduling
sections, effective for all scopes not yet advertised.

### Why it matters to the decision owner

It is written in the Board's native language — coverage, CPE, change-order
exposure, and a deadline — and it never asks them to believe anything a fire
marshal and a commissioning agent wouldn't sign.

### Risk of this frame

Predictability. It is the report the audience expects, and the run prompt's
audience is explicitly tired of it. It also under-uses the ledger's two most
distinctive holdings — the safety reframe and the tenant-audio problem — and
leaves the word "quiet" politically undefended at DCA.

