## Option 2 — Counterintuitive: "The Fire Code Wrote Your Quiet-Airport Standard"

*The most surprising defensible reframing: quiet-by-design is a safety
upgrade wearing a passenger-experience costume — and the fastest way to kill
it is to call it "quiet."*

### Thesis (one sentence)

The strongest case for fewer, better announcements at IAD and DCA is not
passenger comfort but emergency communication: a terminal that broadcasts
constantly cannot be heard when it matters, so the standard MWAA should adopt
is an intelligibility-by-design standard whose most publishable outcome is
safer crisis communication — which is why the fire code, not the branding
deck, already contains most of it.

### Communication job

By the end, airport executives — including the public-safety and emergency-
management chairs who would normally veto anything labeled "quiet" — should
sponsor the standard, because they will understand that today's ambient
acoustic chaos is the thing degrading their ability to move a crowd with a
voice message, and the standard hardens exactly the channels they own.

### Opening scene

Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood, January 6, 2017, minute 91. The shooting is over —
five dead, six wounded at initial contact. Then false reports of a second
shooter ripple through terminals full of people who have received no
authoritative information, and the stampede that follows hospitalizes more
than 50 additional people (E-0193, E-0202, E-0203, E-0204). FLL appears in no
"silent airport" case study. It is the case study. The injuries in hour two
were caused not by a gunman but by the absence of an intelligible,
authoritative voice reaching a crowd already in motion.

### Argument sequence (seven beats)

1. **The FLL stampede** (E-0193, E-0202–E-0204): mass-notification failure,
   not noise, is the terminal-communication problem worth a standard.
2. **The inversion.** The peer "silent airport" canon (LCY, HEL, AMS, CPH,
   BCN — E-0016, E-0017, E-0018, E-0072, E-0061) is policy without a single
   published performance number; the actual numbers live in life-safety code:
   NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 / ≥ 0.45 at 90% of points, verified by STIPA (E-0073,
   E-0074, E-0042). The quiet-airport movement and the fire code are
   converging on the same terminal from opposite directions.
3. **Why constant broadcast degrades safety capacity.** The hospital
   alarm-fatigue record: 86–99.5% of physiologic monitor alarms are false,
   training clinicians to ignore them, which the Joint Commission elevated to
   a National Patient Safety Goal (E-0218, E-0217). Mechanism transfers to PA
   governance; the hospital numbers do not (evidence-map §6). A terminal that
   pages constantly is running the same experiment on its passengers.
4. **ATL, December 17, 2017** (E-0205, E-0206): eleven hours dark, PA and
   FIDS lost with commercial power, megaphones and paper. The standard's
   resilience clauses — head-end UPS, generator runtime independent of
   terminal life-safety power — are safety infrastructure, full stop.
5. **The redundancy floor is federal, not optional.** 49 CFR 27.71 makes
   visual paging a grant-eligibility condition (E-0132); 14 CFR 382.53 pulls
   airline podium audio into scope (E-0133); ADAAG makes audible and visual
   alarms non-swappable (E-0099). "Reduce PA, add screens" is not a lawful
   design; "govern PA, harden every channel" is.
6. **The DCA overlay.** Post-NTSB final report (E-0110, E-0138), anything
   that reads as reducing safety-communications capacity at DCA fails
   politically before it fails technically — and a missed slot at DCA is
   unrecoverable (E-0115, E-0118). The carve-outs (emergency, security,
   IROPS, directive messaging) are not exceptions to the standard; they are
   its core.
7. **Therefore:** adopt the intelligibility-by-design standard in the Design
   Manual now (E-0029, E-0226), with the same arithmetic as Option 1
   (Exhibits E1, E3, E6) arriving late as confirmation, not as the pitch.

### Strongest objection and where it enters

"This is safety-washing — you are dressing an amenity in a flag." It enters
immediately after the FLL scene (beat 2) and is answered by the NTSB
guardrail and the code itself: the NTSB assigned probable cause to FAA
airspace design, not terminal communications (do-not-claim #14 — the draft
must say this explicitly), and the standard's mandatory tier is drawn from
NFPA 72 and federal accessibility regulation, not from passenger-experience
literature. The safety frame is honest precisely because the numbers are the
fire code's, not the marketing department's.

### Memorable comparison

Hospital alarm fatigue (E-0217, E-0218). Hospitals discovered that the
route to patient safety was fewer, better-governed alarms — eliminating
low-value categories so the remaining ones command action. Airport PA
governance is the same problem: message triage, category elimination,
protected channels. Use the mechanism; refuse the hospital numbers.

### Signature visual

**"Two terminals at minute 91."** A split panel: the ungoverned terminal —
overlapping paging zones, tenant TVs, gate handhelds, ambient SPL high, STI
below floor, and the authoritative message lost in the wash — versus the
governed terminal — zoned PA, tenant override, all-call broadcast reaching
every Acoustically Distinguishable Space at STI ≥ 0.50, with the visual/
digital/staffed channels lit in parallel. The same emergency message enters
both panels; it only arrives in one.

### The airport decision it leads toward

The same Design Manual amendment — but branded, defended, and sequenced as
**intelligibility-by-design**, with the emergency-communication and
accessibility tier presented to the Board first and the announcement-
reduction governance presented as its consequence.

### Why it matters to the decision owner

It converts the standard's most dangerous internal opponents — public safety,
emergency management, and any Board member reading through a post-1/29 DCA
lens — into its sponsors, and it gives the CEO a version of the story that
survives the political altitude at which IAD decisions now happen (E-0005).

### Risk of this frame

Two. First, tonal: opening on FLL and 67 DCA fatalities in the same document
demands restraint; one overwrought sentence and the piece reads as trading on
tragedy. The NTSB guardrail must be explicit and early. Second, scope drift:
the safety spine could crowd out the tiered mandatory/recommended/context-
dependent framework the run prompt requires as the decision output — the
Strategist must land the table, not just the reframe.

