## Option 3 — Operational: "MWAA Doesn't Own the Microphone"

*Anchored in a concrete airport moment: a Concourse E holdroom on an IROPS
evening, fall 2026 — the first night the new standard would be tested, in the
one building MWAA just finished.*

### Thesis (one sentence)

A quiet-by-design standard that governs only MWAA-owned systems regulates
roughly a third of what passengers actually hear, so the standard lives or
dies on the unglamorous work of tenant-audio governance — gate podiums,
club audio, TSA callouts, concession TVs — and the SFO mechanism, not the
SFO headline, is the playbook.

### Communication job

By the end, the COO and the airline-relations owner should understand that
the binding constraint is contractual and operational, not acoustic — and
should direct that the Design Manual amendment be drafted with a tenant-
systems chapter and a Use-and-Lease interface from day one, because a
standard without one is decorative.

### Opening scene

Concourse E, fall 2026, first winter IROPS event. The building is brand new —
14 United gates, direct AeroTrain access, a 40,000-square-foot United Club
(E-0227, E-0234). A ground stop hits. In the holdroom, the loudest instrument
is not MWAA's head-end PA: it is a gate agent's handheld on speaker, layered
over the club's entry audio, a TSA recall announcement, and the concession
TVs that outlived CNN Airport Network's 2021 shutdown but not its
programming discipline (E-0219). The COO brief's estimate: MWAA's head-end
covers 30–40% of the audible surface on a peak day (evidence-map §2.3 —
brief-level estimate, flag as such). MWAA just built the quietest shell in
its inventory and does not control most of the sound inside it.

### Argument sequence (six beats)

1. **The Concourse E scene** (E-0227, E-0234, E-0219): the newest building
   demonstrates that the shell is not the problem.
2. **The 30–40% problem.** Airline podiums, club audio, TSA and CBP callouts,
   concessions TV and retail music sit outside the head-end; four agents
   reach this independently (evidence-map §2.3). Governing only MWAA zones
   moves the meter 3–4 dB, not 30.
3. **The airlines hold rights, and precedent says they use them.** The 2025
   Use & Lease grants airlines broadcast rights inside the leased envelope
   (do-not-claim #10); Denver's quiet-zone initiative was reportedly blocked
   by Southwest (E-0121 — single-source, present as illustrative risk, not
   pattern). The gate-podium ownership split at IAD/DCA is an open diligence
   item (evidence-map §5.5).
4. **The mechanism that worked.** SFO cut individual paging 492 → 261/day
   (–47%) and cumulative duration 145 → 58 minutes (–60%) — not by asking
   nicely, but by physically disabling airline access to boarding-area-wide
   paging and centralizing non-routine announcements at its own desk
   (E-0032, E-0033, E-0054, E-0055). Refuse the self-reported "40% noise /
   90 minutes" headline (do-not-claim #4); keep the audio-matrix and
   workflow change. DXB's version was a 10-month airline-by-airline
   negotiation, two announcements per flight (E-0030, E-0031, E-0057 —
   operator-reported numbers, high-confidence mechanism).
5. **The federal hooks that make tenant scope non-negotiable.** 14 CFR
   382.53 obliges carriers to deliver equal information to passengers with
   vision or hearing impairment (E-0133) — the airline's own compliance
   burden is the lever that brings podium audio inside the standard. 49 CFR
   27.71 does the same for MWAA's visual paging (E-0132).
6. **Therefore:** the Design Manual amendment must carry a tenant-systems
   chapter (fit-out acoustic criteria, re-verification after envelope
   penetration, TV/audio governance) and an explicit Use-and-Lease
   interface — and IAD, where United co-branded the transformation, is
   where tenant cooperation gets built first; DCA's slot economics raise
   the announcement floor and get the variance clause (E-0115, E-0118,
   E-0027, E-0028).

### Strongest objection and where it enters

"If MWAA doesn't own the microphone, the standard is futile — you have just
argued against your own thesis." It enters at beat 3, at maximum force, and
is answered by beats 4–5: two operators solved exactly this problem with
identifiable mechanisms (matrix control, centralized desk, per-flight caps,
carrier-by-carrier negotiation), and federal accessibility law gives MWAA
leverage it has not yet used. The gap is scoped work, not impossibility.

### Memorable comparison

SFO's audio matrix as the load-bearing artifact: the quiet airport was not
achieved by a policy memo but by changing who could key which loudspeakers —
governance made physical. (This option needs no cross-domain analogy; the
airport-to-airport mechanism comparison is stronger than any import.)

### Signature visual

**"Who owns the sound in a holdroom."** A plan-view slice of one Concourse E
holdroom with every audio source mapped and color-coded by controlling party
— MWAA head-end, airline podium, airline club, TSA, concessions TV — sized
by estimated contribution, with the MWAA-controlled share visibly the
minority. Beneath it, the same map after the standard: same sources, now
inside one governance envelope with override hierarchy marked.

### The airport decision it leads toward

Direct the Design Manual amendment to include the tenant-systems chapter and
commission the two internal diligence closes that gate it: gate-podium PA
ownership allocation by carrier and terminal, and the Use & Lease
design-manual carve-out language (evidence-map §5.2, §5.5).

### Why it matters to the decision owner

It is the only frame that tells the COO and airline-relations owner the truth
about where this program gets hard, before the Board is asked to promise
results the head-end alone cannot deliver.

### Risk of this frame

It leads with the thesis's biggest vulnerability, and a skimming reader may
exit at beat 3 convinced the standard is futile. The 30–40% figure is a
brief-level operating estimate, not a ledger-verified measurement — it must
be flagged as such or the Fact-checker will strike the opening. And the
frame under-serves the safety and accessibility material that gives the
standard its political armor.

