### Selection: Option 2 — "The Fire Code Wrote Your Quiet-Airport Standard"

Option 2 wins because it is the only frame that solves the run's actual
political problem while staying entirely inside the ledger. The evidence map
records near-unanimous convergence (§2.6) that "quiet" is a bad flag at
post-1/29 DCA and that the standard must be defended as intelligibility-by-
design with safer emergency communication as its most publishable outcome —
Option 2 is that finding turned into a narrative spine. Its surprise is
earned, not decorated: FLL, ATL, NFPA 72, and the alarm-fatigue mechanism
are all high-confidence ledger holdings, and the frame answers the
Contrarian's "no new requirement" objection structurally, since integration
across code, accessibility law, and commissioning is the story itself.

Option 1 scores highest on decision power, so the Strategist should
transplant its spine — the timing window, the Exhibit E1/E6 arithmetic, the
Design Manual vehicle — into Option 2's back half as beats 6–7 and the
closing tiered table. Option 3's holdroom scene and "who owns the sound"
visual should survive as the mid-article reality check on scope. The
discipline required: state the NTSB guardrail early, keep the FLL scene
restrained, and land the mandatory/recommended/context-dependent table the
run prompt demands.
